<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet component="quiz">
    <title>Learning Target 9, 10, 11, 12 Quiz - MTH 204</title>

    <page>
        <p>
            This quiz covers course learning target #9, #10, #11, and #12.
            Read all <term>DIRECTIONS</term> carefully.
            Good luck!
        </p>

        <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Learning Target 12, First Attempt</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can find and clearly justify bases for the null space and
                    the column space of a matrix (including why the set of
                    vectors is a basis, i.e., why it is linearly independent and
                    spans the relevant spaces) and thus their dimensions.
                </p>

                <p>
                        Consider the matrix
                        <md>
                            A = \left[\begin{array}{rrrrrr} 
                            0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp -4 \amp 0 \amp 6 \\
                            0 \amp -4 \amp -1 \amp 7 \amp 0 \amp -16 \\
                            0 \amp 6 \amp 0 \amp -12 \amp 3 \amp 15 \\
                            0 \amp 4 \amp -1 \amp -9 \amp 0 \amp 8
                             \end{array}\right] \sim 
                             \left[\begin{array}{rrrrrr} 
                            0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
                            0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \\
                            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
                            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 
                            \end{array}\right].
                        </md>
                    </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="2.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find a basis for the column space <m>\col(A)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="2.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                       Find a basis for the null space <m>\nul(A)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Complete the sentences:
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <m>\col(A)</m> is a
                        <fillin characters="5"/>-dimensional subspace of
                        <m>\real^p</m> where <m>p=</m> <fillin characters="5"/>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <m>\nul(A)</m> is a
                        <fillin characters="5"/>-dimensional subspace of
                        <m>\real^q</m> where <m>q=</m> <fillin characters="5"/>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Learning Target 11, Second Attempt</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can find the determinant of a given matrix using cofactor
                    expansion and explain what information this tells us about
                    the invertibility of a matrix.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Use a cofactor expansion to find the determinant of the
                        matrix
                        <md>
                            A = \left[\begin{array}{rrr} 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp k \end{array}\right]
                        </md>
                        Your result should depend on <m>k</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For what values of <m>k</m>, if any, is <m>A</m>
                        invertible?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Learning Target 10, Last Attempt</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can convert a vector given in the standard basis into a
                    vector in another basis and vice versa.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Let <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vvec_{1},\vvec_{2}\}</m> be a basis
                    for <m>\mathbb{R}^{2}</m> where
                    <m>\vvec_{1}=\twovec{2}{1}</m> and
                    <m>\vvec_{2}=\twovec{0}{-1}</m>.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find <m>\xvec</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> if
                        <m>\{ \xvec \} _{\mathcal{B}} = \twovec{3}{-2}</m>.
                        Show your work (including noting anything you did in
                        Sage) and explain your thinking.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find <m>\{ \yvec \} _{\mathcal{B}}</m> if
                        <m>\yvec = \twovec{8}{0}</m>.
                        Show your work (including noting anything you did in
                        Sage) and explain your thinking.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise workspace="1in">
            <title>Learning Target 9, Last Attempt</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    I can determine whether or not a set of vectors is a basis
                    for <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>, and give examples and non-examples
                    of bases.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Find a set of vectors from among
                    <md>
                    \vvec_1 = \threevec{2}{-4}{0}, 
                    \vvec_2 = \threevec{0}{-1}{-1},
                    \vvec_3 = \threevec{-4}{7}{-1},
                    \vvec_4 = \threevec{3}{4}{1}
                    </md>
                    that form a basis for <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
                    </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </page>
</worksheet>